course: dictionary
lesson: 12096
title: Spotting codswallop
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Codswallop is an informal word for nonsense: something silly that is not true. You will hear it in Britain, Australia and New Zealand. «That's a load of codswallop!» means "that's complete nonsense!" The words around it show you this.
explain: Adults often use it when a story, excuse or claim sounds like rubbish. «Sam said aliens ate his homework. "That's utter codswallop," said Mum.» Mum does not believe the excuse, so codswallop must mean nonsense. Swap in "nonsense" and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Codswallop is not rude, so you might hear it from grandparents, teachers or on TV. It is a fun word with a useful job: it tells the listener that a claim has no proof behind it. Next time someone says something surprising, ask yourself: is there any evidence for this?
fact: Codswallop is a noun. It means nonsense, something silly and untrue.
fact: It is informal, so it belongs in friendly, chatty speech rather than formal writing.
fact: It is used mainly in the UK, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: «That's a load of codswallop!» means "that's complete nonsense!"
fact: Phrases include «utter codswallop» and «complete codswallop».
exercise: «That's a load of codswallop!» What does codswallop mean? | A kind of bird | A loud noise | *Nonsense, something silly and untrue | Delicious food
exercise: «The advert said the bread would make you grow taller. Dad said, "That's utter codswallop."» What does Dad think of the advert? | It is a true and important fact | *It is silly and not true | It is a funny poem | It is written in French
exercise: Which word means the same as codswallop? | Sunshine | *Nonsense | Treasure | Breakfast
test: «Grandma said the story about a flying pig was complete codswallop.» What does complete codswallop mean? | A tasty pudding | A very long journey | *Totally silly and untrue | A small, shy animal
test: Which word is an informal word for nonsense? | *Codswallop | Pencil | Lantern | Gravel
exam: Mia says, «The moon is made of cheese.» Why might you call that codswallop? | Because it is written in capitals | Because it is a lovely, true fact | *Because it is silly and untrue, with no proof | Because cheese is always silly
